$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{e^{5x-3}}{\ln(x-2)}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[e^{5x-3}\right]}{\dfrac{d}{dx}[\ln(x-2)]}\qquad\gray{\text{l’Hôpital’s rule}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{5e^{5x-3}}{\left(\dfrac{1}{x-2}\right)}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}5(x-2)e^{5x-3}
\\\\
&=\infty
\end{align}$

Note that we were only able to use l’Hôpital’s rule because the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[e^{5x-3}\right]}{\dfrac{d}{dx}[\ln(x-2)]}$ can actually be determined.
